ally a  fully cybernetic body she's not a robot  she's got a human consciousness in there  but she is a completely artificial body  and she's reached a point when she  started asking a lot of philosophical  questions about herself like I have  these  memories and I have this belief that I  have a soul or what they referred to as  a ghost but is it real anymore because  that's the only thing that theoretically  makes me any different from just a  machine and she's asking these questions  about herself while she's helping track  down this criminal the puppet master and  just sort of the meaning of this  existential crisis with the sorts of  crimes that she's tried game down for it  all builds very well now I'm talking the  head of your concepts here initially  because there they are very much front  and center characters actually stop and  talk about some of the philosophical  implications of what is going on but  this is also an anime action movie and  the action is phenomenal it's brutal  really there's some really hard hitting  fights hand a hand gun combat there's  some gory deaths in this but what's  really interesting about it is even when  the action ramps up and if you would  avoid the action in isolation you kind  of get that your house sense to it but  the tone is still very somber I really  thought I chalk that up to the music the  music that is laid in especially during  the action scenes just keeps you in the  philosophical mindset even as you're  watching bullets flying especially in  the climax of the movie the music used  during the climax out which is a piece i  believe called floating museum is one of  my favorite pieces of music written for  the screen and the way it offsets  encounter points what you're watching  it's so not the kind of music you would  expect but it's perfect for what the  film has been building to up to this  point now obviously I've just kind of  been gushing the whole time up to this  do I have anything to complain about not  really there's a couple of nitpicks the  first is if you're gonna watch this as  an American or as an English speaker you  really need to watch it with the  subtitles because the dub the dub sucks  and you need to realize I it takes a lot  for me to recommend watching with the  subtitles on an animated film because  yet when it comes to live-action don't  watch the dub you know they're trying to  recruit not only to the lips not match  up but you know an actor in a booth with  just a microphone was trying to recreate  what the actor live on the set you know  felt and brought to the camera and  that's just not the same but when you're  dealing with animation the original  Japanese  actor was just a guy in a booth so you  can have an English voiceover actor  who's just a guy to booth give a good  proper voiceover the major fall down is  the woman who they got to do major  Motoko Kusanagi and she's just bad  especially when she has to talk for a  while about the philosophical concepts  it just doesn't work now that may end up  being a turn-off for some people that  I'm going to recommend watching with  subtitles because that is distracting  but I think it gets away with in this  film because when they do more talking  tends to be quieter tone down scenes but  you know the more action-oriented sees  tends to just be the action there's not  a lot of talking going on so I think it  works watching it with the subtitles and  I suppose the other thing is you just  got to be in the right headspace this is  a more philosophical piece it's got a  lot of slower paced moments it's still  an action movie it's got great action  set pieces and it does build to like I  said a phenomenal climax that i love but  this is not a rollercoaster of a movie  so if that's what you go in expecting  you're going to come away disappointed  probably annoyed so that's my general  feelings on the filming you can consider  that my spoiler free review getting into  ghost in the shell two point oh I am  going to more freely talk about spoilers  because I have to to talk about some of  the differences between the films I  before i get into the spoilers i will  say goes to the show two point oh don't  bother it is it is like star wars  special editions there's all these  changes that were made for either no  reason or a reason that I don't think is  enough to justify it and they didn't  even take the opportunity to redo a new  American dub on it skip it watch the  original getting into some of the  specifics there's a lot of changes that  went on that and some are just odd like  most of the computer stuff in the  original one that had a green tint to it  which is kind of typical certainly at  this point it's become almost a cliche  they've redone that to an orange tint  and then it's not like that Rex anything  but that's just an odd thing to change  especially because it changes the feel  of it Green is a very cool color orange  is a warm color that fundamentally  changes your emotional reaction to what  you're seeing some of the things they  did a lot of vehicles that were a  hand-drawn animation have been replaced  with CGI vehicles and that I'm its okay  its not an improvement  but it's not horribly integrated there  are a couple of scenes though where  major Kusanagi herself is replaced with  a CGI character and I don't understand  why at all the opening intro they do  this and then at the midpoint of the  movie where she's diving coming out of  the water they do it again and it it  looks wrong especially since the rest of  the movie she doesn't look like that but  when they insert in the middle  especially it looks like you know what  it looks like it looks like a cut scene  out of a video game it just looks wrong  it's jarring and there was nothing wrong  with the original animation there was  one change that they did that I found  kind of interesting but I still don't  like it and that was to the voice actor  doing the puppet master they went from a  male vo